## Why This Is Asked
This question probes practical thinking for real-time services, focusing on WebSocket handshakes, pub/sub latency, and backpressure—common beginner pitfalls when scaling.

## Key Concepts
- WebSocket handshake overhead
- Publish/subscribe latency
- Backpressure and queue depth
- Simple workload ramping
- Component isolation for bottleneck diagnosis

## Code Example
```javascript
// Skeleton k6 WebSocket test
import ws from 'k6/ws';
import { check } from 'k6';
export default function () {
  const res = ws.connect('wss://chat.example.test/ws', {}, function (socket) {
    socket.on('open', function () {
      socket.send(JSON.stringify({ type: 'msg', text: 'hello' }));
    });
  });
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you determine if latency is in handshake vs Redis vs DB?
- What would your SLO look like for 99th percentile latency under peak load?

